    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Vespasian built the Colosseum on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>'s artificial lake, funding it from the spoils of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-jewish-roman-war">First Jewish–Roman War</a>, and placed it at the heart of the city rather than on its outskirts.</li><li>It hosted gladiatorial shows, animal hunts, executions and other spectacles for centuries; gladiatorial fights are last mentioned around 435 and animal hunts continued until at least 523.</li><li>Earthquakes and stone robbers ruined much of it, but from 1749 the popes treated it as a sacred site of the martyrs, protected it and began its long restoration.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Spectators were given tickets in the form of numbered pottery shards, and the passageways that let them reach and leave their seats in minutes were called vomitoria, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> word for a rapid discharge.</p></aside>
    </section>

